From: Neutron lab (ps60b, v2 ps60d)
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: thread pool workers are reused last-in-first-out and retire after 2 minutes of pool idle, as on Windows

What the reporter saw (Neutron#60, reporter's words): "Photoshop 27.8 hangs when switching back after saving a PSD as
PDF on Wayland" / "Photoshop stops responding when I switch back. Easily reproducible." (runtime 217, ntsync off).

Mechanism (SOLID, lab: Photoshop 27.11 on 217 and 229, ntsync on and off, 96 dpi; live TEB stacks, hang report v2,
a +threadpool trace, AdobePDFL/Photoshop.exe disassembly):
- Photoshop runs "Save As > Photoshop PDF" as work items on a PRIVATE thread pool (CreateThreadpool, no minimum).
- The worker that ran the save holds Adobe PDF Library per-thread state. Its destructor runs at thread exit from
  Photoshop.exe's TLS callback (MSVC __dyn_tls_dtor), inside LdrShutdownThread with the loader lock held. It shuts
  down PDFL's own worker threads and waits (INFINITE) for their handles. Those threads block in their own
  LdrShutdownThread on the loader lock: a permanent deadlock. The main thread's next loader call (dxgi Present on
  the repaint after the focus change) blocks too, which is what the reporter sees on switching back.
- Wine retired an idle pool worker after 5 s (THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT), and the pool's last worker too when the
  pool has no objects, so the deadlock started 5-10 s after every PDF save, focus change or not (stock 217/229:
  16 of 16 runs hung).

Windows (measured on the owner's Windows 11 box, probe tpidle.exe, round 53):
- LIFO reuse: 20 single work items 0.5 s apart ran on ONE thread, 20 of 20 on the thread that ran the previous item
  (Wine: 8 threads, 0 of 20). Windows workers wait on the worker factory's I/O completion port, which releases its
  waiters last-in-first-out.
- Idle lifetime ~2 minutes: a default pool and a CreateThreadpool pool, 5 workers each, all alive at 0.5 s idle;
  the first exit at 121.8-122.3 s, all gone by 125.4 s (the 3 s between is the probe's own thread-detach wait under
  the loader lock). Wine: every worker gone 5-8 s after it went idle.
- Workers that had been idle ~11 s longer than the rest (the cold default-pool workers LIFO never reused) retired at
  the same moment as the others: the idle time counts from the POOL's last activity, not per thread.
- The last worker of a pool with no objects exits too (0 of 5 left): Windows does not keep a last worker forever.
- A retiring worker's thread-detach callbacks run under the loader lock on Windows as well (the probe's wait timed
  out). The lock rule is the same; what differs is worker lifetime and reuse.

Fix (dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c):
1. Idle workers wait on their own condition variable, pushed on a per-pool idle stack; new work wakes the top of
   the stack (the most recently idle worker). Shutdown wakes all.
2. The pool records when work was last queued or finished (last_active). An idle worker may retire only once the
   whole pool has been idle for THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS (120 s); a worker woken by its timeout earlier
   re-waits for the remainder. The existing retire rule (min_workers, last worker exits when the pool has no
   objects) is unchanged, now in one helper (tp_worker_may_retire) used by both the wait and the worker loop.
3. (v2) Retire eligibility is decided BEFORE the wait is chosen. Once the pool has been idle for 120 s, a worker
   that may retire returns at once and exits; a worker that may not (the pool's minimum, or the last worker of a
   pool that still has objects) waits a whole 120 s period again, or until new work wakes it. v1 gave such a worker
   the expired deadline (a 0 ms wait) and it looped at 100 % of a CPU until new work arrived: Illustrator,
   Lightroom and Premiere idled at 5-7 CPUs on v1 (ps60c).
Kill switches (read once per process): NEUTRON_TP_LIFO=0 (oldest waiter first, one shared condition variable, as
before), NEUTRON_TP_WIN_TIMEOUT=0 (5 s per-thread idle timeout, as before).

What stays imperfect / not covered:
- The constant: Windows' first exit landed at 121.8-122.3 s, ours at 120.1 s. 120 s is the round value; whether
  Windows uses ~122 s or 120 s plus exit latency is not pinned (optional probe tpidle2.exe pins it, and whether the
  idle clock is per pool or per thread, from one more Windows run).
- Thread creation throttling of the Windows worker factory (it delays new threads while workers are busy) is not
  modeled; Wine still starts a worker per busy submit.
- If Photoshop leaves that pool idle for more than 2 minutes after a PDF save, the save worker retires and the same
  deadlock follows (Windows has the same lock rule, so it would too). Photoshop posts work to the pool far more often
  in the lab (LAB RESULTS).
- The timer-queue, wait-queue and I/O-queue service threads keep their 5 s timeouts (they run no app callbacks).
- A kept worker that becomes free to retire while it sleeps (the pool's last object closed after the pool had
  already been idle 2 minutes) notices at its next wakeup, up to 120 s later; nothing wakes it when objcount drops.
  Windows' exact behavior for that case is not measured.

Lab (ps60b + ps60d, Photoshop 27.11, 168 dpi desk, base Ps60-b229; lab runtime 11.10-229tq = 229 + this ntdll):
- #60 flow (open PSD, Save As > Photoshop PDF [High Quality Print], Alt+Tab away and back once, >= 5.3 min watched after
  the save): away at +10 s ntsync off x2 / on x2, +150 s off x2 / on x2 = 0 of 8 hung, 0 loader_section timeouts, File menu
  opens after switching back. Same build, NEUTRON_TP_WIN_TIMEOUT=0: HUNG within 35 s (owner wine_threadpool_worker in
  AdobePDFL's join, from Photoshop.exe's TLS callback). v1 (same idle rule) at 96 dpi: 0 of 8 hung; NEUTRON_TP_LIFO=0 not hung.
- Stock 217/229 base rate (ps60): 16 of 16 hung (ntsync off/on, switch/no switch).
- Busy-spin (v1) test, tpspin.exe (pool with an object kept + a pool with minimum 2, 130 s idle, CPU over the next 60 s):
  229 0 %, v1 277 % of one CPU (3 workers at 91-94 % each), v2 0 % (also with NEUTRON_TP_LIFO=0). New work still reaches
  the kept workers at once (200 ms item done 200 ms after submit); a kept worker whose pool loses its last object
  retires 120 s after the pool's last item (229: 5 s).
- tpidle under v2: LIFO 20 of 20, all alive at 0.5 s, first exit 120.1 s, all gone 123.1 s (Windows 122.3 / 125.4);
  NEUTRON_TP_LIFO=0 -> 8 threads / 0 of 20; NEUTRON_TP_WIN_TIMEOUT=0 -> all gone at 5 s.
- Idle CPU 229 vs v2 (n=1 per app unless noted): Lightroom (export + import) main 1.8 vs 1.8 %, Illustrator 3.7/2.9 vs
  3.4/2.8 %, Premiere (3 exports, 2.18 vs 2.19 s each) scope 13 vs 17-18 %, Photoshop (n=2 v2) main 1.3-1.4 vs 1.5-2.3 %.

Thread plateau (SOLID numbers, INFERRED cause; ps60c/ps60d): with this patch the apps hold more threads at idle than
stock -- Photoshop about +95 (339-353 vs 256-266), Illustrator +65..70, Lightroom +33..48, Premiere +14 -- flat or
falling over 5 minutes of idle, never rising. That is the measured Windows rule itself (a worker lives until its POOL
has been idle 120 s, so a pool that gets an item at least every 2 minutes keeps every worker its bursts created)
combined with the missing worker-factory throttling above: Windows' factory would have started fewer workers for the
same bursts. Idle CPU is unchanged within noise (Photoshop n=2 main 1.7 vs 1.9 %, Premiere idle 0.0 % both).

In 11.10-231.1 (hotfix line, release-231.1 = 231 (= 227.2 content) + this + placement): the diff body is ps60d's v2 byte for byte
(dry-run -F0 clean on the 227.1/227.2 tree, threadpool.c identical to 229's). Kill switches as above.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c
@@ -98,6 +98,40 @@
 #define THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT 5000
 #define MAXIMUM_WAITQUEUE_OBJECTS (MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS - 1)
 
+/* Neutron#60: pool workers are reused and retired the way Windows does it.
+ *
+ * Measured on Windows 11 (probe tpidle.exe: a default-pool and a CreateThreadpool pool, 8 short items
+ * each, then idle, every worker's thread handle polled every 0.5 s):
+ *  - new work goes to the most recently idle worker: 20 single items 0.5 s apart all ran on one thread
+ *    (Wine: 8 different threads, 0 of 20 on the previous item's thread). The workers wait on an I/O
+ *    completion port (the worker factory), which releases its waiters last-in-first-out.
+ *  - idle workers live about 2 minutes: all 5 + 5 alive after 0.5 s, the first exit at 121.8-122.3 s, the
+ *    rest right after it (Wine: every worker gone 5-8 s after it went idle). Workers that had been idle
+ *    about 11 s longer than the rest (unused because the LIFO order kept one hot thread) exited at the same
+ *    moment, so the idle time counts from the pool's last activity, not from each thread's own last item.
+ *  - the last worker of a pool with no objects exits too (0 of 5 left), as in Wine.
+ *  - a retiring worker's thread-detach callbacks run under the loader lock on Windows as well.
+ *
+ * A worker that may not retire (the pool's minimum, or the last worker while the pool has objects)
+ * keeps sleeping once the pool's 2 minutes are over; it must not poll the expired deadline.
+ *
+ * Why it matters: Photoshop runs "Save As > Photoshop PDF" on a private pool's worker, and the Adobe PDF
+ * Library state that worker then holds is torn down at thread exit (Photoshop.exe's TLS callback, loader
+ * lock held) by waiting for the library's own threads, which need the loader lock to exit: a deadlock as
+ * soon as that worker retires. Wine retired it 5 s after the save; Photoshop posts work to that pool far
+ * more often than every 2 minutes, so on Windows the worker never retires during a session.
+ *
+ * Kill switches (read once): NEUTRON_TP_LIFO=0 wakes the oldest idle worker again (one shared condition
+ * variable); NEUTRON_TP_WIN_TIMEOUT=0 restores the 5 s per-thread idle timeout. */
+#define THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS 120000
+
+/* an idle worker on its pool's idle stack (most recently idle first), locked via the pool's cs */
+struct threadpool_idle_worker
+{
+    struct list             entry;
+    RTL_CONDITION_VARIABLE  cv;
+};
+
 /* internal threadpool representation */
 struct threadpool
 {
@@ -108,6 +142,10 @@
     /* Pools of work items, locked via .cs, order matches TP_CALLBACK_PRIORITY - high, normal, low. */
     struct list             pools[3];
     RTL_CONDITION_VARIABLE  update_event;
+    /* idle workers waiting for work, most recently idle first, locked via .cs */
+    struct list             idle_workers;
+    /* when work was last queued or finished (ms, queue_current_time), locked via .cs */
+    ULONGLONG               last_active;
     /* information about worker threads, locked via .cs */
     int                     max_workers;
     int                     min_workers;
@@ -1751,6 +1789,123 @@
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
+ *           tp_env_switch_on    (internal)
+ *
+ * Neutron#60 kill switches: TRUE unless the variable is set to a value starting with '0'.
+ */
+static BOOL tp_env_switch_on( const WCHAR *var )
+{
+    UNICODE_STRING name, value;
+    WCHAR buf[8];
+
+    RtlInitUnicodeString( &name, var );
+    value.Buffer = buf;
+    value.Length = 0;
+    value.MaximumLength = sizeof(buf);
+    return RtlQueryEnvironmentVariable_U( NULL, &name, &value ) || !value.Length || buf[0] != '0';
+}
+
+static BOOL tp_lifo_enabled(void)
+{
+    static LONG state = -1;  /* -1 unknown, 0 off, 1 on */
+    if (state < 0) InterlockedCompareExchange( &state, tp_env_switch_on( L"NEUTRON_TP_LIFO" ), -1 );
+    return state;
+}
+
+static BOOL tp_win_timeout_enabled(void)
+{
+    static LONG state = -1;
+    if (state < 0) InterlockedCompareExchange( &state, tp_env_switch_on( L"NEUTRON_TP_WIN_TIMEOUT" ), -1 );
+    return state;
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
+ *           tp_wake_idle_workers    (internal)
+ *
+ * Wake the most recently idle worker, or all of them. Called with pool->cs held.
+ */
+static void tp_wake_idle_workers( struct threadpool *pool, BOOL all )
+{
+    struct threadpool_idle_worker *idle;
+    struct list *ptr;
+
+    if (!tp_lifo_enabled())
+    {
+        if (all) RtlWakeAllConditionVariable( &pool->update_event );
+        else RtlWakeConditionVariable( &pool->update_event );
+        return;
+    }
+    while ((ptr = list_head( &pool->idle_workers )))
+    {
+        idle = LIST_ENTRY( ptr, struct threadpool_idle_worker, entry );
+        list_remove( &idle->entry );
+        list_init( &idle->entry );
+        RtlWakeConditionVariable( &idle->cv );
+        if (!all) break;
+    }
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
+ *           tp_worker_may_retire    (internal)
+ *
+ * Whether an idle worker may exit without violating the pool's limits: the pool keeps
+ * min_workers threads, and at least one while it has objects. Called with pool->cs held.
+ */
+static BOOL tp_worker_may_retire( struct threadpool *pool )
+{
+    return pool->num_workers > max( pool->min_workers, 1 ) ||
+           (!pool->min_workers && !pool->objcount);
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
+ *           tp_wait_for_work    (internal)
+ *
+ * An idle worker waits for work on top of the pool's idle stack. Called with pool->cs held.
+ * Returns STATUS_TIMEOUT when the worker may retire: with the Windows timeout, only once the
+ * whole pool has had no work for THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS.
+ *
+ * With the Windows timeout a worker waits until the pool has been idle for that long. Once it
+ * has, a worker that may retire returns at once; one that may not (the pool's minimum, or the
+ * last worker of a pool that still has objects) waits a whole period again, or until woken,
+ * instead of polling the expired deadline.
+ */
+static NTSTATUS tp_wait_for_work( struct threadpool *pool )
+{
+    struct threadpool_idle_worker idle;
+    LARGE_INTEGER timeout;
+    ULONGLONG ms = THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT, idle_ms;
+    NTSTATUS status;
+
+    if (tp_win_timeout_enabled())
+    {
+        idle_ms = queue_current_time() - pool->last_active;
+        if (idle_ms < THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS)
+            ms = THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS - idle_ms;
+        else if (tp_worker_may_retire( pool ))
+            return STATUS_TIMEOUT;
+        else
+            ms = THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS;
+    }
+    timeout.QuadPart = ms * -10000;
+
+    if (!tp_lifo_enabled())
+        status = RtlSleepConditionVariableCS( &pool->update_event, &pool->cs, &timeout );
+    else
+    {
+        RtlInitializeConditionVariable( &idle.cv );
+        list_add_head( &pool->idle_workers, &idle.entry );
+        status = RtlSleepConditionVariableCS( &idle.cv, &pool->cs, &timeout );
+        list_remove( &idle.entry );  /* no-op when a waker already took it off the stack */
+    }
+
+    /* work reached the pool while this worker slept further down the stack: not idle yet */
+    if (status == STATUS_TIMEOUT && tp_win_timeout_enabled() &&
+        queue_current_time() - pool->last_active < THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT_WINDOWS)
+        status = STATUS_SUCCESS;
+    return status;
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
  *           tp_threadpool_alloc    (internal)
  *
  * Allocates a new threadpool object.
@@ -1761,6 +1916,8 @@
     struct threadpool *pool;
     unsigned int i;
 
+    tp_lifo_enabled();  /* resolve the kill switches outside pool->cs */
+    tp_win_timeout_enabled();
     pool = RtlAllocateHeap( GetProcessHeap(), 0, sizeof(*pool) );
     if (!pool)
         return STATUS_NO_MEMORY;
@@ -1775,6 +1932,8 @@
     for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(pool->pools); ++i)
         list_init( &pool->pools[i] );
     RtlInitializeConditionVariable( &pool->update_event );
+    list_init( &pool->idle_workers );
+    pool->last_active = queue_current_time();
 
     pool->max_workers             = 500;
     pool->min_workers             = 0;
@@ -1801,7 +1960,7 @@
     assert( pool != default_threadpool );
 
     pool->shutdown = TRUE;
-    RtlWakeAllConditionVariable( &pool->update_event );
+    tp_wake_idle_workers( pool, TRUE );
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
@@ -2097,12 +2256,13 @@
     /* Count how often the object was signaled. */
     if (object->type == TP_OBJECT_TYPE_WAIT && signaled)
         object->u.wait.signaled++;
+    pool->last_active = queue_current_time();
 
     /* No new thread started - wake up one existing thread. */
     if (status != STATUS_SUCCESS)
     {
         assert( pool->num_workers > 0 );
-        RtlWakeConditionVariable( &pool->update_event );
+        tp_wake_idle_workers( pool, FALSE );
     }
 
     RtlLeaveCriticalSection( &pool->cs );
@@ -2438,7 +2598,6 @@
 static void CALLBACK threadpool_worker_proc( void *param )
 {
     struct threadpool *pool = param;
-    LARGE_INTEGER timeout;
     struct list *ptr;
 
     TRACE( "starting worker thread for pool %p\n", pool );
@@ -2462,6 +2621,7 @@
 
             assert(pool->num_busy_workers);
             pool->num_busy_workers--;
+            pool->last_active = queue_current_time();
 
             tp_object_release( object );
         }
@@ -2475,10 +2635,8 @@
          * decreased without violating the min_workers limit. An exception is when
          * min_workers == 0, then objcount is used to detect if the last thread
          * can be terminated. */
-        timeout.QuadPart = (ULONGLONG)THREADPOOL_WORKER_TIMEOUT * -10000;
-        if (RtlSleepConditionVariableCS( &pool->update_event, &pool->cs, &timeout ) == STATUS_TIMEOUT &&
-            !threadpool_get_next_item( pool ) && (pool->num_workers > max( pool->min_workers, 1 ) ||
-            (!pool->min_workers && !pool->objcount)))
+        if (tp_wait_for_work( pool ) == STATUS_TIMEOUT &&
+            !threadpool_get_next_item( pool ) && tp_worker_may_retire( pool ))
         {
             break;
         }
